  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nA strong upper ridge centered over the southern Rockies and southern\nPlains continues to support  extreme heat across northeast Kansas, \nwith highs around 100\u2013101 and heat index values near 110 this \nafternoon. The Extreme Heat Warning will continue through this \nevening. \n\nUpstream convection today is focused farther northwest and \nwest of the forecast area, where the SPC Day 1 Outlook maintains a \nSlight Risk across portions of the central High Plains including far \nnorthwest Kansas, tied to one or more MCS clusters developing along \nthe northern periphery of the ridge. Guidance favors these complexes \ntracking mainly across western Nebraska and adjacent Kansas, with \nonly low confidence in any eastward extension of stronger winds into \nnorth\u2011central Kansas. Storm chances are very low through the evening.\n\nOvernight, a weak boundary begins pushing southward into our area. \nThis, combined with an increasing low-level jet may be enough to \nspark some scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms as early as \n5am across north central KS, with increasing chances/coverage later \nin the morning across eastern Kansas. Forecast soundings indicate a \ndecoupled boundary layer and dry air just above the surface. The \nthreat for severe storms looks minimal with these features in place \nand less than 1,000 J/Kg of effective CAPE and less than 30 knots of \neffective shear. The slow-moving boundary, increasing cloud cover and\npotential rainfall should all help keep temperatures in check \ntomorrow, with heat indices generally around 100 degrees. \n\nThe upper-level ridge breaks down further by Wednesday and Tuesday\u2019s\nboundary moves off to our south. This should keep us cooler through \nthe remainder of the week, with several chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. Thursday into Friday, a stronger cold front is favored\nto move through, this will bring our highest chances of \nprecipitation for the week (50+%). Given the limited large\u2011scale \nforcing over northeast Kansas relative to areas closer to the main \nnorthern stream shortwave and the modest frontal convergence, \nthunderstorm coverage is expected to be scattered, with QPF generally\nlight to moderate and many locations likely remaining under an inch \nthrough the period, though localized higher amounts are possible \nunder slower or training cells. WPC\u2019s Excessive Rainfall Outlook \nhighlights broader areas of concern for flash flooding but does not \nindicate any focused, higher\u2011category risk centered over northeast \nKansas at this time, supporting a low flash flood threat locally. \n\nThe severe potential late week appears minimal, with SPC \nmedium\u2011range guidance emphasizing stronger instability and shear \ncorridors closer to the northern Plains and Midwest rather than \nnortheast Kansas. With warm mid\u2011level temperatures and only modest \nshear, storms in the Topeka area should mainly pose risks of brief \nheavy rain, lightning, and localized gusty winds, though an isolated \nstronger cell cannot be ruled out, especially Thursday night into \nFriday when the front is closest. Overall, the main impacts for the \nlocal area are a break from the most extreme heat, increased cloud \ncover, and episodic, mainly sub\u2011severe thunderstorms.",
